{"step_index":210,"source":"MODEL","type":"GENERIC","status":"DONE","created_at":"2026-09-30T12:13:41Z","content":"Created At: 2026-09-30T17:43:41+05:30\nCompleted At: 2026-09-30T17:43:41+05:30\nFile Path: `file:///home/ashutosh-goyal/.gemini/antigravity/brain/706cfd11-eeab-4003-9f4b-593d4e66ac26/.system_generated/steps/208/content.md`\nTotal Lines: 144\nTotal Bytes: 6097\nShowing lines 60 to 144\nThe following code has been modified to include a line number before every line, in the format: <line_number>: <original_line>. Please note that any changes targeting the original code should remove the line number, colon, and leading space.\n60:           <a href=\"https://app.codecrafters.io/join?via=cstack\"><img src=\"https://cstack.github.io/db_tutorial/assets/images/code-crafters.jpeg\" alt=\"\"/></a>\n61:         </div>\n62:       </header>\n63:       <section>\n64: \n65:       \n66:       \n67: \n68:       <h1>Part 3 - An In-Memory, Append-Only, Single-Table Database</h1>\n69:       \n70:           <p><a class=\"prev\" href=\"/db_tutorial/parts/part2.html\">Part 2 - World's Simplest SQL Compiler and Virtual Machine</a> <br></p>\n71:       \n72:       \n73:           <p><a class=\"next\" href=\"/db_tutorial/parts/part4.html\">Part 4 - Our First Tests (and Bugs)</a> <br></p>\n74:       \n75: \n76:       <p>We’re going to start small by putting a lot of limitations on our database. For now, it will:</p>\n77: \n78: <ul>\n79:   <li>support two operations: inserting a row and printing all rows</li>\n80:   <li>reside only in memory (no persistence to disk)</li>\n81:   <li>support a single, hard-coded table</li>\n82: </ul>\n83: \n84: <p>Our hard-coded table is going to store users and look like this:</p>\n85: \n86: <table>\n87:   <thead>\n88:     <tr>\n89:       <th>column</th>\n90:       <th>type</th>\n91:     </tr>\n92:   </thead>\n93:   <tbody>\n94:     <tr>\n95:       <td>id</td>\n96:       <td>integer</td>\n97:     </tr>\n98:     <tr>\n99:       <td>username</td>\n100:       <td>varchar(32)</td>\n101:     </tr>\n102:     <tr>\n103:       <td>email</td>\n104:       <td>varchar(255)</td>\n105:     </tr>\n106:   </tbody>\n107: </table>\n108: \n109: <p>This is a simple schema, but it gets us to support multiple data types and multiple sizes of text data types.</p>\n110: \n111: <p><code class=\"language-plaintext highlighter-rouge\">insert</code> statements are now going to look like this:</p>\n112: \n113: <div class=\"language-plaintext highlighter-rouge\"><div class=\"highlight\"><pre class=\"highlight\"><code>insert 1 cstack foo@bar.com\n114: </code></pre></div></div>\n115: \n116: <p>That means we need to upgrade our <code class=\"language-plaintext highlighter-rouge\">prepare_statement</code> function to parse arguments</p>\n117: \n118: <div class=\"language-diff highlighter-rouge\"><div class=\"highlight\"><pre class=\"highlight\"><code>   if (strncmp(input_buffer-&gt;buffer, \"insert\", 6) == 0) {\n119:      statement-&gt;type = STATEMENT_INSERT;\n120: <span class=\"gi\">+    int args_assigned = sscanf(\n121: +        input_buffer-&gt;buffer, \"insert %d %s %s\", &amp;(statement-&gt;row_to_insert.id),\n122: +        statement-&gt;row_to_insert.username, statement-&gt;row_to_insert.email);\n123: +    if (args_assigned &lt; 3) {\n124: +      return PREPARE_SYNTAX_ERROR;\n125: +    }\n126: </span>     return PREPARE_SUCCESS;\n127:    }\n128:    if (strcmp(input_buffer-&gt;buffer, \"select\") == 0) {\n129: </code></pre></div></div>\n130: \n131: <p>We store those parsed arguments into a new <code class=\"language-plaintext highlighter-rouge\">Row</code> data structure inside the statement object:</p>\n132: \n133: <div class=\"language-diff highlighter-rouge\"><div class=\"highlight\"><pre class=\"highlight\"><code><span class=\"gi\">+#define COLUMN_USERNAME_SIZE 32\n134: +#define COLUMN_EMAIL_SIZE 255\n135: +typedef struct {\n136: +  uint32_t id;\n137: +  char username[COLUMN_USERNAME_SIZE];\n138: +  char email[COLUMN_EMAIL_SIZE];\n139: +} Row;\n140: +\n141: </span> typedef struct {\n142:    Statem\n143: \n144: \nThe above content does NOT show the entire file contents. If you need to view any lines of the file which were not shown to complete your task, call this tool again to view those lines.\n"}
